#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-handover-checks: the checks under "Before a handover, run these" in
docs/skill-conventions.md, every one but the run of the workflow's steps, each
held against the answer it has when it is green, and with them the references
between the documents under docs/ that a program can hold, read in the lines
a branch adds or changes.

Usage
    scripts/devloop-handover-checks              every check, in the
                                                 repository this file stands in
    scripts/devloop-handover-checks --all        the same, the references read
                                                 in every line of the
                                                 documents and not in the
                                                 lines of the branch alone
    scripts/devloop-handover-checks --self-test  every outcome once, in a
                                                 throwaway directory outside
                                                 the repository

Exit 0 where every check is green; 1 where one is red; 2 where the program
cannot run: an argument it does not take, a directory above it that is not
devloop's repository, a tool its commands name that the path they run with
does not hold, a list that cannot be read, a question about the commit the
branch forked from that git does not answer. The last line says which of the
first two: it opens with "green:" or with "red:" and carries the count of the
green checks and of the red ones, the red ones by name. Where it cannot run it
prints one line on stderr that says why, and no such last line.

What a check is. One call of check() below: a name, the sentence that says
what it holds, and for all but three its command and the answer that is
green. The command is the
one of the command block the section carried for the check, word for word
but for two, decided on 23 November 2026: default-branch counts in a second
line the files that carry the opening line of the block it checksums, since
four checksums of nothing are one line too; locked-skills prints every place
that names a locked skill, grep -n, where the block printed every file,
grep -l, since a second place in a file its list carries would otherwise go
unseen. The run of the workflow's steps, scripts/devloop-run-workflow, is
none of them: it runs the workflow that runs this program. The three without
a command, entry-heads, entry-letters and plan-lines, hold the references
between the documents: the program reads the documents itself for them, as
the paragraphs from "The references between the documents" to "Which lines
are read" say, and a find of theirs is a place where a reference breaks.

How a command runs. With bash --noprofile --norc -c, in the root of the
repository, in the environment env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin leaves. So grep, sed
and awk are the system's programs and no alias or function of a session's
shell, no locale of a session decides what awk counts or how a list sorts, and
a check answers in a session of Claude Code what it answers in a terminal and
in the workflow. The commands need bash, python3 and git and what POSIX gives
beside them, and no GNU tool: the program runs on macOS as on ubuntu-latest.
What the program asks git for the references it asks in that environment too.

What is green. Two kinds of answer.
- A fixed one: silence, a count, two empty lists, one line per skill. The
  lines a command prints are held against it with the blanks around them
  taken off, since wc pads its count on macOS and not under GNU, and in no
  order. Where an answer depends on the number of the skills, the program
  counts the files skills/*/SKILL.md, so that this number stands in no
  second place.
- A list of lines read. The checks on invocability, on locked skills, on
  offers, on handovers and on a second statement of the unattended finish
  print lines somebody has to judge, and a program judges none. Each has a
  file under docs/handover-checks/, named by the check, that carries the
  lines the check prints which were read and found right, one a line, as the
  command prints it with the line number taken out: FILE:TEXT for a line of
  grep -n, FILE:COUNT for the line of grep -c that invocability prints. Equal
  is green. A line the command prints that the list does not carry is new, a
  line of the list that the command prints no more is gone; either is red and
  printed, and the branch that changed it reads it against the paragraph of
  its check and brings the list in line. In such a file a line that starts
  with # and an empty line are no line of the list.
In both kinds a line on stderr is red: a command that printed an error has
not answered, and silence behind an error is how a check comes to look as if
it passed. A check on the references is green where it finds no break in the
lines it read.

The references between the documents. Three checks, decided on 22, 23 and 24
November 2026, docs/plan.md under milestone 15, where the reasons stand with
what was not chosen.
- entry-heads: an entry that a document names by its head stands exactly
  once. An entry is a line of docs/roadmap.md, or of an archive of it,
  docs/roadmap-archive-<n>.md, that opens with "- **" at the margin, with
  what follows to the line before the next such line or the next heading
  "## "; its head is the text to the closing "**", read across line breaks,
  every run of blanks one blank. The heads are read in docs/roadmap.md under
  every heading and in an archive under "## Known gaps" alone: the sections
  without a date stay in docs/roadmap.md, and an archive carries them as the
  copy of the day it was made, so that every entry of theirs would stand
  twice. A document names an entry by its head where a quote follows the
  word entry, Entry, entries or Entries, with "of" and a date between the
  two or not - a date written out, or "that date", "that day" or "the same
  day" for one -, and behind the date a comma or not; behind entries, every
  further quote joined by a comma, "and" or "or" is read as well. The text
  behind the opening quote is held against every head with its closing
  quote, the longest head first, so that a quote inside a head does not end
  the reading. A quote that is a head whole is green where that head stands
  once. A quote that agrees with a head in its first 20 characters and is
  not that head whole is red, and a head without the period it ends on is
  not that head; with one exception: cut with "…" before its closing quote,
  it is green where exactly one head opens with the text before the "…". A
  quote that agrees with no head that far is no head, the Built line of an
  entry or a commit's subject, and the program says nothing of it. And a
  head a branch adds stands nowhere else: a head that stands more often in
  the working tree than at the commit the branch forked from, counted over
  docs/roadmap.md and the archives together, is red where it stands more
  than once, so that an entry that moves into a new archive, standing once
  before and once after, counts as moved.
- entry-letters: a letter or a number of a decision that a document gives an
  entry named so stands in that entry. Held is an identifier in brackets,
  one small letter or a number, that stands before "of entry", "of the
  entry" or "of the roadmap entry": alone, in a list joined by commas, "and"
  or "or", or as an end of a range "(a) to (h)", every member of which is
  held. It stands in the entry where the entry writes it out in brackets or
  writes such a range around it. An entry named by its date and its subject
  has no head a program can read, and its letters are not held.
- plan-lines: docs/plan.md names no line of a file against the rule at its
  head, a file and a section and never a line, a line of an archive of the
  roadmap being the one exception. A line is the word line or lines and a
  number. Its file is the one in backticks right behind it, after "of", "in"
  or "at", else the last one in backticks before it in its paragraph; a file
  is what stands in backticks without a blank and carries a slash or ends on
  a period and an extension. Green where that file is an archive of the
  roadmap; red for any other, one outside the repository at a named version
  too, and where the paragraph names no file.

Which files are read. The sources are the files under docs/ but two kinds:
the lists under docs/handover-checks/, which carry what commands print, and
the archives of the roadmap. An archive never changes, "An archive of the
roadmap never changes" in docs/skill-conventions.md, so a reference in one
could never be mended, and the check roadmap-names reads none either. An
archive is read for its heads and its entries, as what a reference points
at.

Which lines are read. Without --all, the lines the branch adds or changes,
each with its paragraph. The commit the branch forked from is git merge-base
HEAD origin/main, and it is held against the working tree, so that a change
not committed yet is read like a committed one: the lines git diff -U0
--no-renames marks as added in a source, and every line of a source that did
not stand at that commit under its name, whether git tracks it yet or not. A
paragraph is a run of lines that are not empty, begun anew at a line that
opens with "- " or "#" at the margin; in a source that is no Markdown,
docs/stock-take.tsv, every line is one. Every paragraph that carries a line
of the branch is read whole, so that a branch that changes one line of a
paragraph answers for every reference of that paragraph. On main after a
squash merge HEAD is that commit and nothing is read; in the workflow HEAD
is the branch merged onto main, and that commit is origin/main. Where git
does not name it, there being no origin/main or no commit HEAD shares with
it, the program cannot run, exit 2. With --all every line of every source is
read and git is asked nothing, and a head of docs/roadmap.md that stands
more than once is red whichever branch added it: the run that finds the
breaks that stand, once, for the blocks of docs/defects.md, where reading
everything on every pull request would be red on breaks no branch may mend.
The second line the program prints says which of the two it read, and how
many paragraphs.

What a red find carries: the check's name, the file and the line where the
command names them, the text, and what makes it green; a find on a reference
always its file and its line.

What the program does not hold: that a line of a list is right. Whoever wrote
it there read it. Nor, of the references: a head misquoted within its first
20 characters, which nothing tells from a quote that is no head; an entry
named in another form than those above, "the entry above" among them, or by
the field Found of a block of docs/defects.md, which
scripts/devloop-stock-take holds; a line written without the word line; a
paragraph a branch only takes lines out of; and a reference in a line the
branch leaves alone to an entry the branch changes.
"""

import bisect
import collections
import contextlib
import glob
import io
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

PATH = "/usr/bin:/bin"
TOOLS = ("bash", "python3", "git", "grep", "sed", "awk", "cat", "cksum", "sort", "wc", "tail", "find")
LISTS = "docs/handover-checks"
PARAGRAPH = 'the paragraph of this check under "Before a handover, run these" in docs/skill-conventions.md'
STDERR = "the command wrote this to stderr, and a command that printed an error has not answered. Green: it runs without one and prints what is green"
NEW = 'new, the list %s does not carry it. Green: the line is read against %s; where it holds, it goes into the list as "%s", and where it does not, the file is mended'
GONE = "gone, the list %s carries it and the check prints it no more. Green: the change that took it is read against %s, and the line leaves the list"
NUMBER = re.compile(r"^([^:]+):[0-9]+:")
PLACE = re.compile(r"^((?:skills|shared|bin|scripts|hooks|docs)/[^:\s]*)(?::([0-9]+)(?=:))?(?::\s*(.*))?$")
CHECKS = []
DOCS, ROADMAP, THE_PLAN = "docs", "docs/roadmap.md", "docs/plan.md"
ARCHIVE = re.compile(r"^docs/roadmap-archive-[0-9]+\.md$")
KNOWN = "## Known gaps"
OPENS = 20
DAY = r"(?:[0-9]{1,2} (?:January|February|March|April|May|June|July|August|September|October|November|December) [0-9]{4}|that date|that day|the same day)"
NAMED = re.compile(r"\b([Ee]ntry|[Ee]ntries)(?: of %s,?)? \"" % DAY)
FURTHER = re.compile(r"(?:,? and |,? or |, )\"")
CUT = re.compile(r" ?…\"")
SIGN = r"\((?:[a-z]|[0-9]{1,3})\)"
GIVEN = re.compile(r"(%s(?:(?:,? and |,? or | to |, )%s)*),? of (?:the )?(?:roadmap )?$" % (SIGN, SIGN))
ONE = re.compile(r"\(([a-z]|[0-9]{1,3})\)")
RANGE = re.compile(r"\(([a-z]|[0-9]{1,3})\) to \(([a-z]|[0-9]{1,3})\)")
LINE = re.compile(r"\b[Ll]ines? [0-9]+(?:(?:,? and | to |, )[0-9]+)*")
BEHIND = re.compile(r" (?:of|in|at) `([^`\s]+)`")
TICKED = re.compile(r"`([^`\s]+)`")
ENDING = re.compile(r"\.[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]{0,4}$")
HUNK = re.compile(r"^@@ -[0-9]+(?:,[0-9]+)? \+([0-9]+)(?:,([0-9]+))? @@", re.M)
REFERENCES = "the references between the documents: read in "
NO_BRANCH_POINT = "git %s ended with %d%s: without that answer the lines this branch adds cannot be told from the lines that stood. The checks on the references read them against the commit the branch forked from, git merge-base HEAD origin/main, so origin/main has to be here and share a commit with HEAD; --all reads every line and asks git nothing"
NOT_WHOLE = 'the quote opens as the head at %s:%d opens and is not that head: behind "%s" the head %s and the quote %s. Green: the head whole, as it stands there, its last character with it; or cut with … behind an opening no other head has'
CUT_WIDE = "the quote is cut with … behind an opening that %d heads have, at %s. Green: the quote goes on until one head alone opens so, or the head whole"
TWICE = "the head stands %d times, at %s, and names no single entry. Green: where one of them stands in docs/roadmap.md, that entry gets a head that stands nowhere else"
ADDED = "the head stands %d times, at %s%s. Green: a head for the entry in docs/roadmap.md that stands nowhere else in it and in its archives; an entry that moves into a new archive leaves docs/roadmap.md"
NO_LETTER = 'the entry at %s:%d carries no (%s), written out or inside a range "(x) to (y)" of its own. Green: the letter or number the entry gives the decision, or the entry that carries it'
NO_LINE = "by the rule at the head of docs/plan.md a reference names a file and a section, never a line, a line of an archive docs/roadmap-archive-<n>.md being the one exception. Green: the section in place of the line"
READ = {}  # what one reading of the references found, by check, and under "note" what it read: verdicts() puts it here, judge() and run() take it out, so that a run reads the documents once for the three checks


class CannotRun(Exception):
    pass


def skills(root):
    return sorted(os.path.relpath(p, root).replace(os.sep, "/") for p in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, "skills", "*", "SKILL.md")))


def nothing(root, got):
    return []


def openings(root, got):
    # one line per skill: the opening before any heading, in start-work under the heading on how to talk
    return ["%s: opening %s" % (f, 'under "## How to talk while doing all this"' if f == "skills/start-work/SKILL.md" else "before any heading") for f in skills(root)]


def one_notice(root, got):
    # one line of cksum, whatever its number, and the count of the skills
    one = len(got) == 2 and re.match(r"^[0-9]+ [0-9]+$", got[0])
    return [got[0] if one else "one line of cksum", str(len(skills(root)))]


def check(key, says, where, command, kind, green=None, want=nothing, read=PARAGRAPH):
    CHECKS.append({"key": key, "says": says, "where": where, "command": command.strip("\n"), "kind": kind, "green": green, "want": want, "read": read})


check("registered",
      "Every skill on disk is registered, and every registered skill exists",
      ".claude-plugin/plugin.json and skills/",
      r'''
python3 -c "
import json,pathlib
m=[p.split('/')[-1] for p in json.load(open('.claude-plugin/plugin.json'))['skills']]
d=sorted(p.name for p in pathlib.Path('skills').iterdir())
print('registered but missing:', sorted(set(m)-set(d)))
print('present but unregistered:', sorted(set(d)-set(m)))"
''',
      "answer",
      'both lists empty: every directory under skills/ stands under "skills" in .claude-plugin/plugin.json, and every entry there has its directory',
      want=lambda root, got: ["registered but missing: []", "present but unregistered: []"])

check("roadmap-names",
      "No name in the roadmap resembles a real one without being one",
      "docs/roadmap.md",
      r'''
python3 -c "
import re,pathlib,functools
t=open('docs/roadmap.md').read()
w=set(re.findall(r'\x60([a-z0-9-]*)\x60',t))
d={p.name for p in pathlib.Path('skills').iterdir() if p.is_dir()}
s=t.split('\n## Named, not built as skills\n',1)
r=set(re.findall(r'^\|\s*\x60([a-z0-9-]+)\x60\s*\|',s[1].split('\n## ',1)[0],re.M)) if len(s)==2 else set()
n=d|r
e=functools.lru_cache(None)(lambda a,b:len(a+b) if len(a)==0 or len(b)==0 else min(e(a[1:],b)+1,e(a,b[1:])+1,e(a[1:],b[1:])+1-(a[0]==b[0])))
if len(r)==0 or len(d)==0: print('nothing to hold the roadmap against: %d rows under the table, %d directories under skills/'%(len(r),len(d)))
for x,m in [(x,m) for x in sorted(w-n) for m in sorted(n) if len(m)>=6 and e(x,m)<=2]: print('%s is %d from %s'%(x,e(x,m),m))
"
''',
      "lines",
      'silence: the word is written as the name it was meant to be, or its name gets a directory under skills/ or a row of the table "Named, not built as skills"; and the table carries a row and skills/ a directory')

check("invocability",
      "Invocability is set deliberately, not by omission",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md",
      r'''
grep -c 'disable-model-invocation' skills/*/SKILL.md
''',
      "list",
      read='"## Who may invoke a skill" in docs/skill-conventions.md, which decides for every skill whether a lock stands in its frontmatter, 1, or none, 0')

check("locked-skills",
      "No skill is told to run a skill that is locked against being run",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md and shared/*.md",
      r'''
for s in $(grep -l 'disable-model-invocation' skills/*/SKILL.md | sed 's|skills/||;s|/SKILL.md||'); do
  grep -n "\`$s\`" skills/*/SKILL.md shared/*.md | grep -v "^skills/$s/SKILL.md:"
done
''',
      "list")

check("shared-copies",
      "No shared text still stands written out in a skill",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md",
      r'''
cat shared/*.md | awk 'length >= 40' | grep -Fl -f - skills/*/SKILL.md
''',
      "lines",
      "silence: the copy goes from the skill this line names, which carries a line of forty characters or more of a file under shared/ and inserts that text instead")

check("insert-lines",
      "Every insert line has exactly the form the frontmatter grants, and names a file that exists",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md",
      r'''
grep -n -e '^!`' -e 'devloop-text' skills/*/SKILL.md | awk '{ l=$0; sub(/^[^:]*:[^:]*:/, "", l) } l != "allowed-tools: Bash(${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/devloop-text *)" && l !~ /^!`\$\{CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT\}\/bin\/devloop-text [a-z0-9-]+`$/'
for n in $(grep -ho 'devloop-text [a-z0-9-]*`$' skills/*/SKILL.md | sed 's/devloop-text //;s/`$//' | sort -u); do [ -f "shared/$n.md" ] || echo "no shared/$n.md"; done
''',
      "lines",
      "silence: a line that starts with the insert marker or names bin/devloop-text is the grant or an insert line of the granted form, and the name behind devloop-text is a file under shared/")

check("grant",
      "Every skill that inserts anything carries the grant that lets the program run at load without a prompt",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md",
      r'''
for f in skills/*/SKILL.md; do grep -q '^!`' "$f" && ! grep -qF 'allowed-tools: Bash(${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/devloop-text *)' "$f" && echo "$f: inserts without the allowed-tools line"; done
''',
      "lines",
      "silence: the frontmatter of a skill that inserts carries the line allowed-tools: Bash(${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/devloop-text *)")

check("language-block",
      "The language block stands at its two places in every file",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md",
      r'''
for f in skills/*/SKILL.md; do
  [ "$(tail -n 1 "$f")" = '!`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/devloop-text closing`' ] || echo "$f: does not end on the closing language block"
  awk -v f="$f" '/^## /{h=$0} /devloop-text language-opening`$/{n++; o=NR; print f": opening " (h=="" ? "before any heading" : "under \"" h "\"")} /devloop-text skill-name`$/{m++; if(NR!=o+2) print f": skill-name line not two lines under the opening"} END{if(n!=1) print f": opening block inserted " n+0 " times"; if(m!=1) print f": skill-name line inserted " m+0 " times"}' "$f"
done
''',
      "lines",
      'one line per skill saying where the opening stands, before any heading and in start-work under "## How to talk while doing all this", and nothing else: the closing block is the last line of the file, and the opening and the line on skill names are inserted once each, the second two lines under the first',
      want=openings)

check("notice",
      "The notice above the first insert line is the same paragraph in every skill",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md",
      r'''
for f in skills/*/SKILL.md; do sed -n '/^\*\*If `\[shell command execution disabled by policy\]`/,/^$/p' "$f" | cksum; done | sort -u
grep -l '^\*\*If `\[shell command execution disabled by policy\]`' skills/*/SKILL.md | wc -l
''',
      "answer",
      "one line of cksum and the count of the skills: the paragraph from **If `[shell command execution disabled by policy]` to the next empty line stands in every skill and reads the same in all",
      want=one_notice)

check("expansion",
      "Every skill expands without error",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md",
      r'''
for f in skills/*/SKILL.md; do scripts/devloop-expand "$f" > /dev/null || echo "$f: expansion failed"; done
''',
      "lines",
      "silence: scripts/devloop-expand prints the skill and ends with 0, every insert line naming a file under shared/ and bin/devloop-text running")

check("executable",
      "The program under bin/ and the tools under scripts/ are executable, in the working tree and in what git records, and git tracks every file there",
      "bin/ and scripts/",
      r'''
for p in bin/* scripts/*; do [ -x "$p" ] || echo "$p: not executable"; done; git ls-files -s bin/ scripts/ | grep -v '^100755 '; [ "$(git ls-files bin/ scripts/ | wc -l)" -eq "$(find bin scripts -type f | wc -l)" ] || echo "bin/ or scripts/ has files git does not track"
''',
      "lines",
      "silence: every file under bin/ and scripts/ is executable in the working tree and stands as 100755 in what git records, git update-index --chmod=+x, and git tracks every file there; where it does not, scripts/__pycache__/ is the first place to look")

check("arming-guard",
      "The arming command in the merge guard's message is the one in shared/arming-command.md",
      "hooks/pre-tool-use-merge-guard.sh",
      r'''
sed 's/^    //' shared/arming-command.md | grep -cFx -f - hooks/pre-tool-use-merge-guard.sh
''',
      "answer",
      "1: the line of shared/arming-command.md stands in the hook whole and unchanged, without the four blanks of its indentation",
      want=lambda root, got: ["1"])

check("arming-conventions",
      'The arming command under "Arming auto-merge is allowed; merging is not" in docs/skill-conventions.md is the one in shared/arming-command.md',
      "docs/skill-conventions.md",
      r'''
grep -cFx -f shared/arming-command.md docs/skill-conventions.md
''',
      "answer",
      "1: the line of shared/arming-command.md stands in docs/skill-conventions.md whole and unchanged, indented as in the shared file",
      want=lambda root, got: ["1"])

check("default-branch",
      "The default branch is resolved in bin/devloop-install-record, in bin/devloop-setup-state and in bin/devloop-start-conditions exactly as hooks/pre-tool-use-branch-guard.sh resolves it",
      "bin/devloop-install-record, bin/devloop-setup-state, bin/devloop-start-conditions and hooks/pre-tool-use-branch-guard.sh",
      r'''
for f in bin/devloop-install-record bin/devloop-setup-state bin/devloop-start-conditions hooks/pre-tool-use-branch-guard.sh; do sed -n '/^DEFAULT=\$(git symbolic-ref/,/^fi$/p' "$f" | cksum; done | sort -u | wc -l
grep -l '^DEFAULT=\$(git symbolic-ref' bin/devloop-install-record bin/devloop-setup-state bin/devloop-start-conditions hooks/pre-tool-use-branch-guard.sh | wc -l
''',
      "answer",
      "1 and 4: the lines from DEFAULT=$(git symbolic-ref to the fi that closes the fallback read the same in all four files, and all four carry the line DEFAULT=$(git symbolic-ref; four checksums of nothing are one line too, which is what the 4 holds",
      want=lambda root, got: ["1", "4"])

check("status-forms",
      "The sentence naming the four forms a status takes is, in both table guards, the one in shared/status-forms.md",
      "hooks/post-tool-use-table-guard.sh and hooks/pre-tool-use-table-guard.sh",
      r'''
for f in hooks/post-tool-use-table-guard.sh hooks/pre-tool-use-table-guard.sh; do grep -cFx -f shared/status-forms.md "$f"; done
''',
      "answer",
      "1 twice, the post guard first: the one line of shared/status-forms.md stands whole and unchanged in the message of each",
      want=lambda root, got: ["1", "1"])

check("offers",
      "No sentence tells a run to ask for permission to reach the next stage, or to put a question as an either-or",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md and shared/*.md",
      r'''
grep -rn 'Ask whether\|offer to\|Offer to\|on a yes\|offering the next' skills/*/SKILL.md shared/*.md
''',
      "list")

check("handovers",
      "Every place a run hands the user something to do says where a no leads",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md and shared/*.md",
      r'''
grep -rn 'picks up as soon as\|picks up once\|picks up the moment\|carry on when it has run\|once it has run\|hand them the command\|hand the user the\|Hand the user the' skills/*/SKILL.md shared/*.md
''',
      "list")

check("unattended-finish",
      "The unattended finish is not stated in the readiness query's vocabulary alone",
      "skills/build-work/SKILL.md",
      r'''
for spec in "skills/build-work/SKILL.md:^## Unattended mode"; do
  f=${spec%%:*}; h=${spec#*:}
  awk -v h="$h" '$0 ~ h {n=1;next} n&&/^## /{exit} n' "$f" |
    grep -q 'raised-here' || echo "$f: the unattended finish does not mention loose raised-here issues"
done
''',
      "lines",
      'silence: the section "## Unattended mode" of skills/build-work/SKILL.md names the loose issues by their label, raised-here, and the heading stands as the check names it')

check("second-statement",
      "The decision on the unattended finish is stated once, and no place states it a second time",
      "skills/*/SKILL.md and shared/*.md",
      r'''
grep -rn 'Several: ask\|None: stop\|One ready task: continue\|nothing in scope is ready any more\|nothing ready is left in scope' skills/*/SKILL.md shared/*.md
''',
      "list")

check("entry-heads",
      "An entry that a document names by its head stands exactly once, and a head the branch adds stands nowhere else",
      "docs/",
      "",
      "references",
      "no find: the quote behind entry is a head whole, or one cut with … behind an opening no other head has, of an entry that stands once over docs/roadmap.md and its archives")

check("entry-letters",
      "A letter or a number of a decision that a document gives an entry named by its head stands in that entry",
      "docs/",
      "",
      "references",
      "no find: every identifier in brackets before of entry, of the entry or of the roadmap entry stands in the entry named, written out or inside a range of its own")

check("plan-lines",
      "docs/plan.md names no line of a file but of an archive of the roadmap, by the rule at its head",
      "docs/plan.md",
      "",
      "references",
      "no find: every line docs/plan.md names is a line of an archive docs/roadmap-archive-<n>.md")


def answer(root, c, path):
    """What the command of a check prints, on stdout and on stderr, the empty lines left out."""
    p = subprocess.run([shutil.which("bash", path=path), "--noprofile", "--norc", "-c", c["command"]], cwd=root, env={"PATH": path}, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE)
    return [[l for l in s.decode("utf-8", "replace").split("\n") if l.strip()] for s in (p.stdout, p.stderr)]


def read_list(root, name):
    """The lines of a list: every line of the file but the empty ones and those that start with #."""
    try:
        text = open(os.path.join(root, name), encoding="utf-8").read()
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
        raise CannotRun("the list %s cannot be read: %s" % (name, getattr(e, "strerror", None) or "it is no UTF-8"))
    return [l.rstrip() for l in text.split("\n") if l.strip() and not l.startswith("#")]


def place(line, where):
    """The file, with the line where the command names one, and the text of a line a command printed."""
    line = line.strip()
    m = PLACE.match(line)
    return (m.group(1) + (":" + m.group(2) if m.group(2) else ""), m.group(3) or "") if m else (where, line)


def quoted(lines):
    return ", ".join('"%s"' % l for l in lines) or "nothing"


# ------------------------------------------- the references between the documents

def git(root, path, *args):
    """What git answers to one question about the repository; where it answers none, the program cannot run."""
    p = subprocess.run([shutil.which("git", path=path)] + list(args), cwd=root, env={"PATH": path}, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE)
    if p.returncode:
        said = p.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip().split("\n")[0]
        raise CannotRun(NO_BRANCH_POINT % (" ".join(args), p.returncode, ", " + said if said else ""))
    return p.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").replace("\r\n", "\n")


def documents(root):
    """The lines of every file under docs/ but the lists of the checks, each under its path from the root."""
    out = {}
    for there, dirs, names in os.walk(os.path.join(root, DOCS)):
        rel = os.path.relpath(there, root).replace(os.sep, "/")
        if rel == LISTS:
            del dirs[:]
            continue
        for n in names:
            with open(os.path.join(there, n), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as f:
                out[rel + "/" + n] = f.read().split("\n")
    return out


def entries(rel, lines):
    """The entries of docs/roadmap.md or of an archive: for each its head, the line it opens on and the line it ends on."""
    marks, section = [], None
    for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
        if l.startswith("## "):
            section = l.rstrip()
            marks.append((i, False))
        elif l.startswith("- **") and section and (rel == ROADMAP or section == KNOWN):
            marks.append((i, True))
    out = []
    for k, (i, opens) in enumerate(marks):
        if opens:
            text, j = lines[i - 1][4:], i
            while "**" not in text and j < len(lines):
                text, j = text + " " + lines[j], j + 1
            out.append((" ".join(text.split("**", 1)[0].split()), i, marks[k + 1][0] - 1 if k + 1 < len(marks) else len(lines)))
    return out


def heads(files):
    """Every head with the places it stands at, over docs/roadmap.md and its archives: the file, the line, the last line of the entry."""
    out = {}
    for rel in sorted(files, key=lambda r: (r != ROADMAP, r)):
        if rel == ROADMAP or ARCHIVE.match(rel):
            for head, i, end in entries(rel, files[rel]):
                out.setdefault(head, []).append((rel, i, end))
    return out


def paragraphs(rel, lines):
    """The paragraphs of a source: for each the numbers of its lines, its text with every run of blanks one blank, and where in that text each line begins."""
    runs, cur, table = [], None, not rel.endswith(".md")
    for i, l in enumerate(lines, 1):
        if not l.strip():
            cur = None
            continue
        if cur is None or table or l.startswith(("- ", "#")):
            cur = ([], [])
            runs.append(cur)
        cur[0].append(i)
        cur[1].append(" ".join(l.split()))
    return [(numbers, " ".join(parts), [sum(len(p) + 1 for p in parts[:k]) for k in range(len(parts))]) for numbers, parts in runs]


def span(lo, hi):
    """The members of a range from its first end to its last; the two ends alone where they do not rise as letters or as numbers."""
    if lo.isdigit() and hi.isdigit() and int(lo) < int(hi):
        return [str(n) for n in range(int(lo), int(hi) + 1)]
    if lo.isalpha() and hi.isalpha() and lo < hi:
        return [chr(n) for n in range(ord(lo), ord(hi) + 1)]
    return [lo, hi]


def signs(text):
    """The identifiers a text carries in brackets: each written out, and each inside a range it writes."""
    return set(ONE.findall(text)) | {x for lo, hi in RANGE.findall(text) for x in span(lo, hi)}


def brief(text, most=96):
    return text if len(text) <= most else text[:most - 34].rstrip() + " … " + text[-30:].lstrip()


def places(stands):
    return ", ".join("%s:%d" % (rel, i) for rel, i, end in stands)


def meant_by(rest, order):
    """What the text behind an opening quote names: a head whole, a head cut with …, a head that it opens as and is not, or nothing where it opens as no head opens. With it the heads meant and how far the quote was read."""
    for head in order:
        if rest.startswith(head) and rest[len(head):len(head) + 1] == '"':
            return "whole", [head], len(head) + 1
    best, n = None, 0
    for head in order:
        k = len(os.path.commonprefix([rest[:len(head)], head]))
        if k > n:
            best, n = head, k
    if n < OPENS:
        return None, [], 0
    cut = CUT.match(rest, n)
    if cut:
        return "cut", [head for head in order if head.startswith(rest[:n].rstrip())], cut.end()
    return "other", [best], n


def references(root, path=PATH, everything=False):
    """What the three checks on the references find, by check, each find the place, the text and what makes it green; and the line that says what was read."""
    files = documents(root)
    sources = sorted(rel for rel in files if not ARCHIVE.match(rel))
    now, then, lines_of = heads(files), None, dict.fromkeys(sources)
    if not everything:
        base = git(root, path, "merge-base", "HEAD", "origin/main").strip()
        stood = set(git(root, path, "ls-tree", "-r", "--name-only", base, "--", DOCS).split("\n"))
        then = heads({rel: git(root, path, "show", "%s:%s" % (base, rel)).split("\n") for rel in stood if rel == ROADMAP or ARCHIVE.match(rel)})
        for rel in sources:
            if rel in stood:
                hunks = HUNK.findall(git(root, path, "diff", "--no-ext-diff", "--no-color", "--no-renames", "-U0", base, "--", rel))
                lines_of[rel] = {n for first, count in hunks for n in range(int(first), int(first) + int(count or 1))}
    found = {"entry-heads": [], "entry-letters": [], "plan-lines": []}
    order, carried, read = sorted(now, key=len, reverse=True), {}, collections.Counter()
    for rel in sources:
        for numbers, text, starts in paragraphs(rel, files[rel]):
            if lines_of[rel] is not None and lines_of[rel].isdisjoint(numbers):
                continue
            read[rel] += 1

            def at(offset):
                return "%s:%d" % (rel, numbers[bisect.bisect_right(starts, offset) - 1])

            for m in NAMED.finditer(text):
                pos, first = m.end(), True
                while pos:
                    rest = text[pos:]
                    kind, meant, length = meant_by(rest, order)
                    said = text[pos - len(m.group(0)) if first else pos - 1:pos]
                    if kind == "other":
                        head, n = meant[0], length
                        stop = rest.find('"', n)
                        found["entry-heads"].append((at(pos), said + brief(rest[:stop + 1 if 0 <= stop - n < 40 else n + 20]), NOT_WHOLE % (now[head][0][0], now[head][0][1], rest[max(0, n - 24):n].lstrip(), 'goes on with "%s"' % head[n:n + 16] if head[n:] else "ends", "ends" if stop == n else ('with "%s"' if head[n:] else 'goes on with "%s"') % rest[n:stop if n < stop < n + 16 else n + 16])))
                    elif kind == "cut" and len(meant) > 1:
                        found["entry-heads"].append((at(pos), said + brief(rest[:length]), CUT_WIDE % (len(meant), ", ".join(places(now[head]) for head in meant))))
                    elif kind and len(now[meant[0]]) > 1:
                        found["entry-heads"].append((at(pos), said + brief(rest[:length]), TWICE % (len(now[meant[0]]), places(now[meant[0]]))))
                    elif kind and first:
                        given = GIVEN.search(text[:m.start()])
                        if given:
                            entry, i, end = now[meant[0]][0]
                            if (entry, i) not in carried:
                                carried[entry, i] = signs(" ".join(" ".join(files[entry][i - 1:end]).split()))
                            ranged = [r.span() for r in RANGE.finditer(given.group(1))]
                            asked = {x for r in RANGE.finditer(given.group(1)) for x in span(*r.groups())} | {o.group(1) for o in ONE.finditer(given.group(1)) if not any(a <= o.start() < b for a, b in ranged)}
                            for x in sorted(asked - carried[entry, i], key=lambda x: (len(x), x)):
                                found["entry-letters"].append((at(given.start(1)), '(%s), in "%s" of the entry "%s"' % (x, given.group(1), brief(meant[0], 60)), NO_LETTER % (entry, i, x)))
                    further = FURTHER.match(text, pos + length) if kind in ("whole", "cut") and m.group(1).lower() == "entries" else None
                    pos, first = further.end() if further else 0, False
            if rel == THE_PLAN:
                for m in LINE.finditer(text):
                    behind = BEHIND.match(text, m.end())
                    named = [t for t in TICKED.findall(text[:m.start()]) + ([behind.group(1)] if behind else []) if "/" in t or ENDING.search(t)]
                    if not named:
                        found["plan-lines"].append((at(m.start()), "%s, and its paragraph names no file in backticks before it or right behind it" % m.group(0), NO_LINE))
                    elif not ARCHIVE.match(named[-1]):
                        found["plan-lines"].append((at(m.start()), "%s, a line of `%s`" % (m.group(0), named[-1]), NO_LINE))
    for head, stands in now.items():
        was = len(then.get(head, ())) if then is not None else 0
        if len(stands) > 1 and stands[0][0] == ROADMAP if everything else len(stands) > max(was, 1):
            found["entry-heads"].append(("%s:%d" % stands[0][:2], "- **%s**" % brief(head), ADDED % (len(stands), places(stands), "" if everything else ", and the branch adds it: at the commit it forked from, %s, it stood %s" % (base[:7], "%d times" % was if was != 1 else "once"))))
    if everything:
        return found, REFERENCES + "every line of the %d sources under docs/, %d paragraphs, with --all" % (len(sources), sum(read.values()))
    return found, REFERENCES + "the %d paragraphs of %d sources under docs/ that carry a line this branch adds or changes against %s, git merge-base HEAD origin/main" % (sum(read.values()), len(read), base[:7])


def judge(root, c, path=PATH):
    """The finds of one check, none where it is green: each the place, the text and what makes it green."""
    if c["kind"] == "references":
        held = READ.pop(c["key"], None)
        return references(root, path)[0][c["key"]] if held is None else held
    out, err = answer(root, c, path)
    where, green = c["where"], c["green"]
    finds = [(where, "stderr: " + l.strip(), STDERR) for l in err]
    if c["kind"] == "list":
        name = "%s/%s.txt" % (LISTS, c["key"])
        left = collections.Counter(read_list(root, name))
        for raw in out:
            line = NUMBER.sub(r"\1:", raw.rstrip(), count=1)
            if left[line] > 0:
                left[line] -= 1
                continue
            finds.append(place(raw, where) + (NEW % (name, c["read"], line),))
        return finds + [place(line, where) + (GONE % (name, c["read"]),) for line in sorted(left.elements())]
    got = [l.strip() for l in out]
    want = c["want"](root, got)
    if c["kind"] == "answer":
        return finds + [(where, "prints %s where %s is green" % (quoted(got), quoted(want)), "Green: " + green)] * (sorted(got) != sorted(want))
    left = collections.Counter(want)
    for line in got:
        if left[line] > 0:
            left[line] -= 1
            continue
        finds.append(place(line, where) + ("the check prints this line. Green: " + green,))
    return finds + [place(line, where) + ("the check does not print this line. Green: " + green,) for line in sorted(left.elements())]


def verdicts(root, path=PATH, everything=False):
    """Every check with its finds, in the order of the section, the three on the references last and read once for all three."""
    if not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(root, ".claude-plugin", "plugin.json")) or not skills(root):
        raise CannotRun("the directory above this program is not devloop's repository: it carries no .claude-plugin/plugin.json or no skills/*/SKILL.md")
    missing = [t for t in TOOLS if not shutil.which(t, path=path)]
    if missing:
        raise CannotRun("the commands run with PATH=%s, and it holds no %s" % (path, ", no ".join(missing)))
    READ.clear()
    found, READ["note"] = references(root, path, everything)
    READ.update(found)
    return [(c, judge(root, c, path)) for c in CHECKS]


def run(root, path=PATH, everything=False):
    """Every check once: the exit code, and the lines to print."""
    try:
        found = verdicts(root, path, everything)
    except CannotRun as e:
        READ.clear()
        return 2, ["devloop-handover-checks cannot run: %s" % e]
    lines = ['devloop-handover-checks: %d checks of "Before a handover, run these", each command run as env -i PATH=%s bash --noprofile --norc in the repository this program stands in' % (len(CHECKS), path)]
    lines.append(READ.pop("note"))
    for c, finds in found:
        lines.append("%s  %s: %s" % ("RED" if finds else "ok ", c["key"], c["says"]))
        for where, text, green in finds:
            lines += ["     %s%s" % (where, ": " + text if text else ""), "       " + green]
    red = [c["key"] for c, finds in found if finds]
    last = "red: %d checks green, %d red: %s" % (len(CHECKS) - len(red), len(red), ", ".join(red)) if red else "green: %d checks green, 0 red" % len(CHECKS)
    return int(bool(red)), lines + [last]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- self-test

INSERT = "!`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/devloop-text %s`"
GRANT = "allowed-tools: Bash(${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/devloop-text *)"
NOTICE = "**If `[shell command execution disabled by policy]` stands anywhere in this"
ARM = "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/bin/devloop-arm <number>"
FORMS = "A status is `filled`, `empty` or `skipped (user): <the user's reason>`."
OFFER = "Say what is missing and offer to bring it up to date."
HANDOVER = "The build picks up once the tool stands where that command puts it."
SECOND = "None: stop, the build has it so."
LOCKED = "An armed pull request goes to `start-work` from here."
DEFAULT = '#!/bin/sh\nDEFAULT=$(git symbolic-ref --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD 2>/dev/null)\nif [ -z "$DEFAULT" ]; then\n  DEFAULT=main\nfi\n'
GUARD = "#!/bin/sh\ncat <<'EOF'\n%s\nEOF\n"
EXPAND = r'''#!/bin/sh
# the fixture's stand-in for scripts/devloop-expand: 0 where every insert line of the file names a file under shared/
missing=$(for n in $(sed -n 's/^!`.*devloop-text \([a-z0-9-]*\)`$/\1/p' "$1"); do [ -f "shared/$n.md" ] || echo "$n"; done)
[ -z "$missing" ]
'''
BUILD, PLAN, START = "skills/build-work/SKILL.md", "skills/plan-work/SKILL.md", "skills/start-work/SKILL.md"
RATIONALE, ARCHIVED = "docs/rationale.md", "docs/roadmap-archive-1.md"
THREE = "The plan decided, in three parts."
ANSWER = 'The answer "none" leaves the step, and a guard holds it.'
GATE, GUARDED = "The archive opens with one entry, on the gate.", "The archive opens with one entry, on the guard."
AGAINST = "A wording kept as an example."
LONG = "A head so long that a find of the letters cannot carry it whole, the gate and the guard."
TWO = 'Held in entry "The plan decided, in two parts.".'
SKILL_LINE = "Decided for line 12 of `skills/build-work/SKILL.md`."
WHO = {"GIT_AUTHOR_NAME": "t", "GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL": "t@t", "GIT_COMMITTER_NAME": "t", "GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL": "t@t"}


def skill(name, body, locked=False, under=None):
    """A skill of the fixture: frontmatter with the grant, the notice, the opening and the line on skill names, the body, the closing."""
    opening = [INSERT % "language-opening", "", INSERT % "skill-name", ""]
    top = ["# " + name, "", under, ""] + opening if under else opening + ["# " + name, ""]
    front = ["---", "name: " + name, "description: a skill of the fixture"] + ["disable-model-invocation: true"] * locked + [GRANT, "---", ""]
    return "\n".join(front + [NOTICE, "text, nothing was inserted: say so and stop.**", ""] + top + body + ["", INSERT % "closing"]) + "\n"


def put(T, rel, text, mode=None):
    path = os.path.join(T, rel)
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(text)
    os.chmod(path, mode or 0o644)


def get(T, rel):
    return open(os.path.join(T, rel), encoding="utf-8").read()


def swap(T, rel, old, new):
    text = get(T, rel)
    assert old in text, "the fixture's %s does not carry %r" % (rel, old)
    with open(os.path.join(T, rel), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(text.replace(old, new, 1))


def add(T, rel, line):
    # a line into a skill of the fixture, above its closing block
    swap(T, rel, "\n" + INSERT % "closing", "\n" + line + "\n\n" + INSERT % "closing")


def more(T, rel, text):
    # a paragraph at the end of a document of the fixture
    put(T, rel, get(T, rel) + "\n" + text + "\n")


def tgit(T, *args):
    # git in the fixture, its commits under the fixed identity of the self-tests, which is made up
    return subprocess.run([shutil.which("git", path=PATH)] + list(args), cwd=T, env=dict(WHO, PATH=PATH), check=True, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE).stdout.decode("utf-8")


def commit(T, subject):
    tgit(T, "add", "-A")
    tgit(T, "commit", "-q", "-m", subject)


def fixture(T):
    """The green repository of the self-test, built anew: three skills, the shared texts, the programs and hooks the commands read, the documents with an archive of the roadmap, the lists, a commit of all that with origin/main on it, and in the working tree two paragraphs a branch adds, every reference of theirs holding."""
    READ.clear()
    for n in os.listdir(T):
        shutil.rmtree(os.path.join(T, n))
    put(T, ".claude-plugin/plugin.json", '{"name": "fixture", "skills": ["./skills/build-work", "./skills/plan-work", "./skills/start-work"]}\n')
    put(T, BUILD, skill("build-work", [LOCKED, "", "## Unattended mode", "", "The run ends once nothing is ready and no loose issue labelled `raised-here` is left."]))
    put(T, PLAN, skill("plan-work", [OFFER, "", SECOND]))
    put(T, START, skill("start-work", ["Ask what they would like to build."], locked=True, under="## How to talk while doing all this"))
    put(T, "shared/language-opening.md", "Write in the language of the person.\n")
    put(T, "shared/skill-name.md", "Name a skill by its name.\n")
    put(T, "shared/closing.md", "Close in the same language.\n")
    put(T, "shared/arming-command.md", "    " + ARM + "\n")
    put(T, "shared/status-forms.md", FORMS + "\n")
    put(T, "shared/install-tool.md", HANDOVER + "\n")
    put(T, ROADMAP, "# Roadmap\n\n## Named, not built as skills\n\n| Name | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `tidy-notes` | named, not built |\n\n## Known gaps\n\n- An entry on `build-work`, on `tidy-notes` and on the flag `--auto`.\n- **The plan decided, in three\n  parts.** Decided: (a) to (c). Measured: (1) and (2).\n\n## Decisions taken against\n\n- **%s** Not built.\n" % AGAINST)
    put(T, ARCHIVED, "# Roadmap\n\n## Known gaps\n\n- **%s** It decides\n  (4) and (8).\n- **%s** Old.\n- **%s** Old.\n\n## Decisions taken against\n\n- **%s** Not built.\n" % (ANSWER, GATE, GUARDED, AGAINST))
    put(T, THE_PLAN, "# The plan\n\nA reference below names a file and a section, never a line.\n\nThe counter runs out late, `kernel/pid.c` line 28 at v1,\nand no run meets it.\n")
    put(T, RATIONALE, "# Reasons\n\n- **A rule.** Decided in entry \"%s\".\n" % THREE)
    put(T, "docs/skill-conventions.md", "# Conventions\n\n    " + ARM + "\n")
    put(T, "hooks/pre-tool-use-merge-guard.sh", GUARD % ARM)
    put(T, "hooks/pre-tool-use-table-guard.sh", GUARD % FORMS)
    put(T, "hooks/post-tool-use-table-guard.sh", GUARD % FORMS)
    put(T, "hooks/pre-tool-use-branch-guard.sh", DEFAULT)
    for name in ("install-record", "setup-state", "start-conditions"):
        put(T, "bin/devloop-" + name, DEFAULT, 0o755)
    put(T, "scripts/devloop-expand", EXPAND, 0o755)
    put(T, LISTS + "/invocability.txt", "# the count of the locks per skill\n\n%s:0\n%s:0\n%s:1\n" % (BUILD, PLAN, START))
    put(T, LISTS + "/locked-skills.txt", "%s:%s\n" % (BUILD, LOCKED))
    put(T, LISTS + "/offers.txt", "%s:%s\n" % (PLAN, OFFER))
    put(T, LISTS + "/handovers.txt", "shared/install-tool.md:%s\n" % HANDOVER)
    put(T, LISTS + "/second-statement.txt", "%s:%s\n" % (PLAN, SECOND))
    tgit(T, "init", "-q")
    tgit(T, "symbolic-ref", "HEAD", "refs/heads/main")
    commit(T, "the fixture as main carries it")
    tgit(T, "update-ref", "refs/remotes/origin/main", "HEAD")
    more(T, RATIONALE, '- **Another rule.** Decided as (a) and (c) of entry "The plan decided, in\n  three parts.", with (b) of the roadmap entry of 1 January 2026 "%s".' % THREE)
    more(T, THE_PLAN, "The gate was measured, `%s` lines 7 to 8." % ARCHIVED)


def selftest():
    """Every outcome of the program once, in a throwaway directory outside the repository: every check green and red, a list with a new line and with a line gone, a line on stderr, and each exit code."""
    T = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="devloop-handover-checks-selftest.")
    try:
        return selftest_in(T)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(T, ignore_errors=True)


